  "questionLabels": [],
  "question": {
    "text": "<p>The magnetic field at the centre of a wire loop formed by two semicircular wires of radii <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mo> </mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>π</mi><mo> </mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math> carrying current <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>I</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn><mo> </mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></math> as per figure given below is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>α</mi><mo>×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow></msup><mo> </mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></math>. The value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>α</mi></math> is _____. (Centre <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi></math> is common for all segments)</p><img src=\"https://cdn-question-pool.getmarks.app/pyq/jee_main/a46f2cde-6b61-42cd-875b-aa07113600e9.png\" style=\"width: 250px; height: 182px;\"/>",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [],
  "correctValue": "3",
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p><img src=\"https://cdn-question-pool.getmarks.app/pyq/jee_main/045a49dc-e6d5-4cb3-8f86-5e20e42ef08a.png\" style=\"width: 250px; height: 186px;\"/></p><p>From the figure, it is evident that the magnetic field at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi></math> due to the straight portion of the loop is zero, only the contributions from the circular parts survive.</p><p>Also, the direction of the magnetic field due to the circular loops can be determined by the right-hand corkscrew rule.</p><p>Hence, the net magnetic field at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>O</mi></math> can be calculated as follows:</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mtable columnalign=\"right center left\" columnspacing=\"0px\"><mtr><mtd><mi>B</mi></mtd><mtd><mo>=</mo></mtd><mtd><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mi>i</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mfrac><mfenced><mfrac><mi>π</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi></mrow></mfrac></mfenced><mo>⊗</mo><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mi>i</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mfrac><mfenced><mfrac><mi>π</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi></mrow></mfrac></mfenced><mo>⊗</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd><mo>=</mo></mtd><mtd><mfenced><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mi>i</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mi>i</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mfenced><mo>⊗</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd><mo>=</mo></mtd><mtd><mfrac><mrow><mn>4</mn><mi>π</mi><mo>×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow></msup><mo>×</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>π</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>4</mn><mi>π</mi><mo>×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow></msup><mo>×</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>⊗</mo><mo> </mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd><mo>=</mo></mtd><mtd><mn>3</mn><mo>×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow></msup><mo>⊗</mo><mo> </mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable></math></p><p>Hence, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></math>.</p>",
    "image": null
  },
